Applying the Rydberg formula for hydrogen-like species, the wave number is given by $ ar{
u} = R_H Z^2 \left( rac{1}{n_1^2} -  rac{1}{n_2^2}ight)$. For $	ext{He}^+$ ($Z=2$), the second line of the Balmer series corresponds to $n_1=2$ and $n_2=4$. Substituting these values, we get $ ar{
u}_1 = R_H (2^2) \left( rac{1}{4} -  rac{1}{16}ight) = 4 R_H \left( rac{3}{16}ight) =  rac{3}{4} R_H$. For $	ext{Li}^{2+}$ ($Z=3$), the transition terminates at $n_1=3$ from an initial level $n_x$. Substituting into the Rydberg equation yields $ ar{
u}_2 = R_H (3^2) \left( rac{1}{3^2} -  rac{1}{n_x^2}ight) = 9 R_H \left( rac{1}{9} -  rac{1}{n_x^2}ight) = R_H \left(1 -  rac{9}{n_x^2}ight)$. Equating $ ar{
u}_1 =  ar{
u}_2$, we have $1 -  rac{9}{n_x^2} =  rac{3}{4}$, which implies $ rac{9}{n_x^2} =  rac{1}{4}$, solving to $n_x^2 = 36$ and therefore $n_x = 6$. Forgetting to square the nuclear charge $Z$ leads incorrectly to option C, while confusing the transition series index gives option B or D.
